<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seon Buddhism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Korean Seon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Seon</a> is the Korean form of Chan or Zen Buddhism, which stresses meditation and direct insight. Introduced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> in the eighth and ninth centuries, it spread through the Nine Mountain schools of late Silla. In Goryeo the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinul">Jinul</a> joined it with doctrinal study, and it remains the main tradition of Korean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> through the Jogye Order.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seon-buddhism.jpg" alt="Seon Buddhism" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Anzola · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jogyesa_Temple_(1509839597).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Seon</a> is the Sino-Korean pronunciation of Chan, short for chánnà, a Chinese transliteration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> dhyāna, meditation. Sinhaeng, who studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> with Puji, a successor of Yuquan Shenxiu of the East Mountain Teaching, was among its early teachers in Unified Silla, and Doui popularised it at the beginning of the ninth century. Korean monks, mostly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hwaeom">Hwaeom</a> and Yogacara background, went to China to study under Mazu Daoyi and his successors, and on their return founded schools at mountain monasteries. Eight of the nine mountain schools traced their lineage to Mazu; the exception, the Sumi-san school of Yieom, came from the Caodong school. Doui, founder of the Gaji Mountain school, is regarded as the first patriarch of Seon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>By the eleventh century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Seon</a> had set itself apart from the scholastic schools, called gyo, learning, and tension grew between them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinul">Jinul</a>, the most important figure of Goryeo Seon, answered a sangha seen as corrupted by fortune-telling and rituals for worldly success with a samādhi and prajñā society of disciplined practitioners, centred on Songgwangsa at Jogyesan. Drawing on Guifeng Zongmi and Dahui Zonggao, he taught sudden enlightenment followed by gradual practice and adopted Dahui's gwanhwa, observing the critical phrase or hwadu, still the main meditation taught in Korean Seon. His successor Jingak Hyesim collected 1,125 gongans in the Seonmun yeomsongjip of 1226.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Under Joseon, which embraced neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was suppressed for some 500 years: monasteries fell from several hundred to thirty-six, monks and nuns were barred from the cities, and Buddhist funerals and begging were outlawed. Queen Munjeong was an exception, installing the monk Bou as head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Seon</a> school. During the Japanese invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> (1592–98) the Seon master Hyujeong, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seosan">Seosan</a>, led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> units of monks, and most modern lineages trace back to him through his four main disciples.</p><p>Under Japanese rule most monks were forced to marry. In the 1980s the master Seongcheol revived the slogan of sudden enlightenment and sudden cultivation against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinul">Jinul</a>'s formula. Abroad, Seungsahn founded the Providence Zen Center after moving to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1972, and Samu Sunim founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>'s Zen Buddhist Temple in 1971.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Korean Seon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Goryeo printers also used movable metal type: the Jikji, a collection of <b>Seon Buddhist</b> teachings printed at Heungdeoksa temple in Cheongju in 1377, is the oldest surviving book printed with movable metal type, and literary sources record metal-type printing in Goryeo earlier in the thirteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
